---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'Isn’t amending the Constitution impossible?',
    a: 'It’s hard by design — two-thirds of Congress and three-quarters of the states — and that bar is the organizing principle of this project, not an obstacle to it. It has been met 27 times, almost always for proposals with overwhelming cross-party support. That’s exactly the kind we work on.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Which party is this really for?',
    a: 'Neither. Every proposal must show credible support across the full spectrum — from MAGA Republicans to socialist Democrats — before it makes the list, and we publish the party-by-party polling so you can check us. When a popular idea fails that bar, we reject it and say why.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Why isn’t my issue included?',
    a: 'Probably because it couldn’t pass the full-spectrum test — see the rejected categories for each judgment and the reasoning behind it. Rejection isn’t a judgment that an issue doesn’t matter; it’s a judgment that it can’t yet clear the bar.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Why amendments instead of legislation?',
    a: 'Because the problems we target are structural: they persist precisely because ordinary legislation can’t reach them, or because any statute can be repealed by the next majority. Rules that bind everyone, permanently, belong in the Constitution.',
  },
  {
    q: 'If an amendment passes, what happens to the laws already on the books?',
    a: 'Laws that conflict with it stop being enforceable the day it is certified, though the text stays in the statute books until repealed; laws consistent with it stand. Some provisions work by themselves; others leave a piece to Congress or the states to fill in by statute. Legislatures can also prepare in advance with conforming or contingent laws. The general rules are on <a href="/amendments/after-ratification">After ratification</a>, and each amendment page has a section on its own federal and state law today and what changes the day after.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How is this funded?',
    a: 'Right now, it isn’t — the work is volunteer. Founding donors ($1,000+) will fund the foundation’s formal launch: incorporation, 501(c)(3) filing, and a founding board. No money is collected until the entity and its bank account exist.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How can I help?',
    a: 'We need volunteers for research, analytics, polling, salon organizing, and fundraising. Tell us how you can help and why you believe in the mission on the <a href="/get-involved/volunteer">volunteer page</a>. If you can commit $1,000 or more, the <a href="/get-involved/seed-donors">founding donor round</a> is what starts the foundation itself.',
  },
];
---

<Base title="FAQ" description="Recurring questions about the New Bill of Rights project, answered.">
  <div class="container">
    <h1>FAQ</h1>
    <dl>
      {
        faqs.map((f) => (
          <>
            <dt>{f.q}</dt>
            <dd set:html={f.a} />
          </>
        ))
      }
    </dl>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  dt {
    font-family: var(--font-serif);
    font-size: var(--step-1);
    margin-top: var(--space-3);
  }

  dd {
    margin: var(--space-1) 0 0;
    max-width: var(--measure);
  }
</style>
